jection handling (76/100; weight 25%): \"When a customer raised price, I asked what budget they had and reviewed the basic package. My manager approved a smaller scope. I did not promise a discount independently. The customer kept talking with us, but I did not personally own the closing decision.\"","Q4 - Client handoff (72/100; weight 20%): \"I send an email with the contract and contact names to the account team. I try to attend the kickoff. I do not yet use a standard checklist for risks or customer success measures; our account manager fills those in.\""],"limitations":["Limited direct ownership evidence; no inference about capability outside the specific examples provided.","Synthetic scores are illustrative, not real assessment results.","All communication, audio, distraction, confidence and risk signals are synthetic display examples. No real audio/video was analyzed."],"risk":{"integrity_risk":"Medium","reason":"Simulated risk example only; no integrity assessment of a real person occurred."}}},{"id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000011003","candidate_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000001003","client_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000000001","role_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000000100","transcript":"[SYNTHETIC DEMO TRANSCRIPT - no real interview was conducted]\n\nQ1 - Interviewer: Describe a discovery call that changed your sales approach. What did you learn and what did you do?\nTaylor Reed: I usually explain the features first and ask whether the price works. In retail I helped someone compare two products. I have not run a business discovery call or mapped decision-makers, so I would need a process and coaching.\n\nQ2 - Interviewer: How do you manage your pipeline and forecast? Give a concrete example.\nTaylor Reed: I keep a personal list and contact people when I have time. I have not used a formal opportunity pipeline or forecast model. I would mark a deal likely when a customer seemed interested, but I cannot provide an accuracy example.\n\nQ3 - Interviewer: Tell me about a price objection and how you handled it without overpromising.\nTaylor Reed: I would tell the customer we are the best and try to get a lower price approved. I do not yet know how to compare staffing service levels. I would ask my manager before making a guarantee or offering a discount.\n\nQ4 - Interviewer: How do you hand a newly won account to the delivery team?\nTaylor Reed: I would send the buyer information to the next team. I have not done an account kickoff and cannot give an example of documenting delivery risks. A checklist would help me know what to include.","status":"completed","is_active":false,"attempt_number":1,"attempt_mode":"video","created_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","started_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","ended_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","has_substantive_response":true,"substantive_response_count":4,"candidate_utterance_count":4,"transcript_available":true,"recording_status":"demo_placeholder","video_url":null,"transcript_scores":{"overall":51,"confidence":46,"ai_aided_risk":"high","ai_aided_risk_reason":"Simulated demo signal for follow-up examples only, not evidence of AI use by a real person.","rubric_scores":[{"category":"Discovery and qualification","weight":30,"score":52,"evidence":"Q1 - Discovery and qualification (52/100; weight 30%): \"I usually explain the features first and ask whether the price works. In retail I helped someone compare two products. I have not run a business discovery call or mapped decision-makers, so I would need a process and coaching.\""},{"category":"Pipeline discipline","weight":25,"score":48,"evidence":"Q2 - Pipeline discipline (48/100; weight 25%): \"I keep a personal list and contact people when I have time. I have not used a formal opportunity pipeline or forecast model. I would mark a deal likely when a customer seemed interested, but I cannot provide an accuracy example.\""},{"category":"Objection handling","weight":25,"score":54,"evidence":"Q3 - Objection handling (54/10