t: A customer had lost access before a deadline. I acknowledged the impact, repeated the goal, and gave a twenty-minute update commitment rather than promising an immediate fix. I checked access logs, involved the account administrator, and sent updates until restored. I documented the cause and confirmed they could complete the task.\n\nQ2 - Interviewer: Walk me through diagnosing a customer who cannot access their account.\nCasey Bennett: I first check the exact error, URL, and whether others are affected. I verify identity using our approved process, never ask for a password, and check account state and service health. If a safe browser check fails, I record the steps and escalate with a redacted screenshot. I verify the customer can sign in before closing.\n\nQ3 - Interviewer: How do you prioritize competing tickets and decide when to escalate?\nCasey Bennett: I prioritize safety or access-wide incidents, then business impact and SLA. Yesterday I linked six duplicate reports to one outage and escalated once with affected counts. A single how-to question received an update while I worked the incident. I documented owners and next update times.\n\nQ4 - Interviewer: What do you record in case notes and what information should you avoid collecting?\nCasey Bennett: I record the reported issue, timestamps, troubleshooting, outcome, and next owner. I exclude passwords, authentication tokens, and unnecessary personal information. Screenshots are redacted. For a suspected security issue I use the restricted escalation route instead of copying details broadly.","status":"completed","is_active":false,"attempt_number":1,"attempt_mode":"video","created_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","started_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","ended_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","has_substantive_response":true,"substantive_response_count":4,"candidate_utterance_count":4,"transcript_available":true,"recording_status":"demo_placeholder","video_url":null,"transcript_scores":{"overall":93,"confiden