. Yesterday I linked six duplicate reports to one outage and escalated once with affected counts. A single how-to question received an update while I worked the incident. I documented owners and next update times.\"","Q4 - Documentation and privacy (94/100; weight 20%): \"I record the reported issue, timestamps, troubleshooting, outcome, and next owner. I exclude passwords, authentication tokens, and unnecessary personal information. Screenshots are redacted. For a suspected security issue I use the restricted escalation route instead of copying details broadly.\""],"limitations":["Authored synthetic evidence, not a verified operational support history or media-based evaluation.","Synthetic scores are illustrative, not real assessment results.","All communication, audio, distraction, confidence and risk signals are synthetic display examples. No real audio/video was analyzed."],"risk":{"integrity_risk":"Low","reason":"Simulated risk example only; no integrity assessment of a real person occurred."}},"unanswered_candidate_questions":["Validate case-quality examples and discuss adapting the process to staffing-client workflows."],"analysis":{"scores":{"response_specificity":93,"answer_directness":95,"answer_consistency":94,"communication_structure":93},"conditions":{"evaluation_conditions":"Good","signal_confidence":"High","audio_quality_issues":"None","distraction_risk":"Low"},"evidence_summary":"[SYNTHETIC DEMO] Strong evidence. Explains troubleshooting, impact-based triage, and privacy-safe documentation in a clear sequence. The simulated presentation is confident and attentive; validate case-quality examples in follow-up. Validate case-quality examples and discuss adapting the process to staffing-client workflows.","evidence":["Q1 - Customer communication (93/100; weight 30%): \"A customer had lost access before a deadline. I acknowledged the impact, repeated the goal, and gave a twenty-minute update commitment rather than promising an immediate fix. I checked access logs, involved the account administrator, and sent updates until restored. I documented the cause and confirmed they could complete the task.\"","Q2 - Troubleshooting (92/100; weight 30%): \"I first check the exact error, URL, and whether others are affected. I verify identity using our approved process, never ask for a password, and check account state and service health. If a safe browser check fails, I record the steps and escalate with a redacted screenshot. I verify the customer can sign in before closing.\"","Q3 - Prioritization and escalation (91/100; weight 20%): \"I prioritize safety or access-wide incidents, then business impact and SLA. Yesterday I linked six duplicate reports to one outage and escalated once with affected counts. A single how-to question received an update while I worked the incident. I documented owners and next update times.\"","Q4 - Documentation and privacy (94/100; weight 20%): \"I record the reported issue, timestamps, troubleshooting, outcome, and next owner. I exclude passwords, authentication tokens, and unnecessary personal information. Screenshots are redacted. For a suspected security issue I use the restricted escalation route instead of copying details broadly.\""],"limitations":["Authored synthetic evidence, not a verified operational support history or media-based evaluation.","Synthetic scores are illustrative, not real assessment results.","All communication, audio, distraction, confidence and risk signals are synthetic display examples. No real audio/video was analyzed."],"risk":{"integrity_risk":"Low","reason":"Simulated risk example only; no integrity assessment of a real person occurred."}}},{"id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000012002","candidate_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000002002","client_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000000001","role_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000000200","transcript":"[SYNTHETIC DEMO TRANSCRIPT - no real interview was conducted]\n\nQ1 - Interviewer: Describe a frustrated customer interaction and how you responded.\nRiley Chen: A customer was upset about an order delay. I acknowledged it and checked the order, then explained the next delivery estimate. I followed up the next day. I did not have a written update schedule, but the customer thanked me for checking back.\n\nQ2 - Interviewer: Walk me through diagnosing a customer who cannot access their account.\nRiley Chen: I ask what error they see and suggest trying another browser. If that fails I send it to the lead. I have not routinely checked service health or account state myself. I would not ask for their password, and I would record the exact error.\n\nQ3 - Interviewer: How do you prioritize competing tickets and decide when to escalate?\nRiley Chen: I usually work oldest tickets first, but would move an urgent issue up if my lead asked. I do not yet use a formal impact matrix. Once I escalated several related tickets separately and later learned we should link them under one incident.\n\nQ4 - Interviewer: What do you record in case notes and what information should you avoid collecting?\nRiley Chen: I write the issue and what I tried in the ticket. I never record a password. I am still learning which screenshots require redaction and when a case should go to the restricted security queue.","status":"completed","is_active":false,"attempt_number":1,"attempt_mode":"video","created_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","started_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","ended_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","has_substantive_response":true,"substantive_response_count":4,"candidate_utterance_count":4,"transcript_available":true,"recording_status":"demo_placeholder","video_url":null,"transcript_scores":{"overall":75,"confidence":71,"ai_aided_risk":"low","ai_aided_risk_reason":"Simulated demo signal for follow-up examples only, not evidence of AI use by a real person.","rubric_scores":[{"category":"Customer communication","weight":30,"score":80,"evidence":"Q1 - Customer communication (80/100; weight 30%): \"A customer was upset about an order delay. I acknowledged it and checked the order, then explained the next delivery estimate. I followed up the next day. I did not have a written update schedule, but the customer thanked me for checking back.\""},{"category":"Troubleshooting","weight":30,"score":72,"evidence":"Q2 - Troubleshooting (72/100; weight 30%): \"I ask what error they see and suggest trying another browser. If that fails I send it to the lead. I have not routinely checked service health or account state myself. I would not ask for their password, and I would record the exact error.\""},{"category":"Prioritization and escalation","weight":20,"score":69,"evidence":"Q3 - Prioritization and escalation (69/100; weight 20%): \"I usually work oldest tickets first, but would move an urgent issue up if my lead asked. I do not yet use a formal impact matrix. Once I escalated several related tickets separately and later learned we should link them under one incident.\""},{"category":"Documentation and privacy","weight":20,"score":76,"evidence":"Q4 - Documentation and privacy (76/100; weight 20%): \"I write the issue and what I tried in the ticket. I never record a password. I am still learning which screenshots require redaction and when a case should go to the restricted security queue.\""}]},"perception_scores":{"unavailable":true,"mode":"demo","reason":"No recording or perception analysis exists for this synthetic example.","synthetic":true,"clarity":80,"confidence":75,"engagement":83},"interview_summary":"[SYNTHETIC DEMO] Mixed evidence. Provides understandable customer updates and basic browser checks, but needs stronger escalation and prioritization structure. The simulated presentation is engaged with moderate confidence; use a triage exercise to test process maturity. Use a triage exercise to test business-impact prioritization and privacy-safe escalation notes.","interview_analysis_v2":{"scores":{"response_specificity":75,"answer_directness":77,"answer_consisten