require redaction and when a case should go to the restricted security queue.\""],"limitations":["Specific gaps concern process maturity, not unobserved personal traits.","Synthetic scores are illustrative, not real assessment results.","All communication, audio, distraction, confidence and risk signals are synthetic display examples. No real audio/video was analyzed."],"risk":{"integrity_risk":"Low","reason":"Simulated risk example only; no integrity assessment of a real person occurred."}}},{"id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000012003","candidate_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000002003","client_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000000001","role_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000000200","transcript":"[SYNTHETIC DEMO TRANSCRIPT - no real interview was conducted]\n\nQ1 - Interviewer: Describe a frustrated customer interaction and how you responded.\nMorgan Blake: I would apologize and tell the customer I will try to fix it. I once redirected a visitor to the right office. I do not have a detailed example of managing a frustrated support customer through resolution.\n\nQ2 - Interviewer: Walk me through diagnosing a customer who cannot access their account.\nMorgan Blake: I might ask them to send a screenshot and tell me what they typed. I have not worked with a formal identity-check process. I now understand that I should not request passwords and would ask a supervisor for the approved troubleshooting steps.\n\nQ3 - Interviewer: How do you prioritize competing tickets and decide when to escalate?\nMorgan Blake: I would answer whichever ticket I opened first unless someone called again. I cannot give an example of prioritizing a service outage or using an SLA. I would need training on the escalation rules.\n\nQ4 - Interviewer: What do you record in case notes and what information should you avoid collecting?\nMorgan Blake: I usually write that I talked to the customer and whether I passed it on. I have not used a technical case-note template or redacted a screenshot. 