entative | Summit Field Partners (fictional) | 2020-2022 Built qualified outbound meetings and maintained CRM decision-maker and next-step records. Follow-up: Validate quota attainment with references and ask how the approach scales across several concurrent accounts."},"interview_breakdown":{"summary":"[SYNTHETIC DEMO] Strong evidence. Clear, structured examples connect discovery, qualification, and delivery. The simulated presentation is confident and engaged; validate the claimed outcomes in follow-up. Validate quota attainment with references and ask how the approach scales across several concurrent accounts.","rubric_scores":[{"category":"Discovery and qualification","weight":30,"score":94,"evidence":"Q1 - Discovery and qualification (94/100; weight 30%): \"A regional client asked for twenty placements in four weeks. I asked about coverage gaps, attrition, and manager availability. The real constraint was weekend coverage, not headcount. I mapped the five critical shifts and brought delivery into a scoping call. We proposed eight initial placements with weekly checkpoints instead of promising twenty. The first cohort covered all five shifts and the client expanded after six weeks.\""},{"category":"Pipeline discipline","weight":25,"score":92,"evidence":"Q2 - Pipeline discipline (92/100; weight 25%): \"Every Friday I inspect stage age, a dated next step, the decision process, and a customer-confirmed timeline. In Q3 I removed two verbal commitments from commit because procurement had not approved terms. That reduced my forecast from $310,000 to $245,000; we closed $238,000. My manager could plan delivery capacity using the smaller, defensible number.\""},{"category":"Objection handling","weight":25,"score":90,"evidence":"Q3 - Objection handling (90/100; weight 25%): \"A buyer said our fee was eighteen percent above another offer. I clarified the scope and showed our agreed service levels, not unsupported competitor claims. I offered a smaller pilot at the published rate rather than a discount I could not authorize. The buyer selected the pilot. I documented the assumptions and did not guarantee a hiring outcome.\""},{"category":"Client handoff","weight":20,"score":91,"evidence":"Q4 - Client handoff (91/100; weight 20%): \"Before handoff I review the signed scope, contacts, success measures, promised timelines, and open risks with delivery. The client joins a kickoff. For one account I highlighted a pending security review and assigned an owner before work began. I stayed through the first checkpoint and confirmed the customer understood the next steps.\""}],"synthetic":true,"clarity":94,"confidence":92,"engagement":95,"evaluation_reliability":94,"ai_aided_risk":"low","conditions":{"evaluation_conditions":"Good","signal_confidence":"High","audio_quality_issues":"None","distraction_risk":"Low"},"integrity_risk":"Low"},"analysis":{"summary":"[SYNTHETIC DEMO] Strong evidence. Clear, structured examples connect discovery, qualification, and delivery. The simulated presentation is confident and engaged; validate the claimed outcomes in follow-up. Validate quota attainment with references and ask how the approach scales across several concurrent accounts.","evidence":["Q1 - Discovery and qualification (94/100; weight 30%): \"A regional client asked for twenty placements in four weeks. I asked about coverage gaps, attrition, and manager availability. The real constraint was weekend coverage, not headcount. I mapped the five critical shifts and brought delivery into a scoping call. We proposed eight initial placements with weekly checkpoints instead of promising twenty. The first cohort covered all five shifts and the client expanded after six weeks.\"","Q2 - Pipeline discipline (92/100; weight 25%): \"Every Friday I inspect stage age, a dated next step, the decision process, and a customer-confirmed timeline. In Q3 I removed two verbal commitments from commit because procurement had not approved terms. That reduced my forecast from $310,000 to $245,000; we closed $238,000. My manager could plan delivery capacity using the smaller, defensible number.\"","Q3 - Objection handling (90/100; weight 25%): \"A buyer said our fee was eighteen percent above another offer. I clarified the scope and showed our agreed service levels, not unsupported competitor claims. I offered a smaller pilot at the published rate rather than a discount I could not authorize. The buyer selected the pilot. I documented the assumptions and did not guarantee a hiring outcome.\"","Q4 - Client handoff (91/100; weight 20%): \"Before handoff I review the signed scope, contacts, success measures, promised timelines, and open risks with delivery. The client joins a kickoff. For one account I highlighted a pending security review and assigned an owner before work began. I stayed through the first checkpoint and confirmed the customer understood the next steps.\""],"limitations":["Results are self-reported within an authored demonstration. No employer verification or recorded interview exists.","Synthetic scores are illustrative, not real assessment results.","All communication, audio, distraction, confidence and risk signals are synthetic display examples. No real audio/video was analyzed."]},"unanswered_candidate_questions":["Validate quota attainment with references and ask how the approach scales across several concurrent accounts."]},{"id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000021002","candidate_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000001002","client_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000000001","role_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000000100","interview_id":"d38ade00-2026-4000-8000-000000011002","attempt_number":1,"report_kind":"complete_interview","created_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","report_generated_at":"2026-10-07T21:00:00.000Z","resume_score":76,"interview_score":74,"overall_score":75,"resume_breakdown":{"experience_match_percent":77,"skills_match_percent":80,"education_match_percent":70,"resume_score":76,"summary":"[SYNTHETIC DEMO]